d vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186381985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d1f0a35-56cb-5370-99fa-b599f7ccb0a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381985 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186381986 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381986 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381986 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381986 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381986 #edb/ref 1